      "feedback": "1. Critical Botanical & Ecological Corrections\nSeed Dispersal & Evolutionary Context:\n\nThe Issue: The profile states that birds and insects avoid the fruit and claims \"humans are the primary dispersal agents\" or that it is an \"outlier.\"\n\nThe Correction: Canistel is not an outlier—it exhibits a classic mammal dispersal syndrome (large fruit, yellow-orange color, thick rind, heavy seed, dense aromatic pulp). In its native lowland Neotropical habitat, seed dispersal is driven by arboreal and terrestrial mammals (such as primates, bats, and opossums), rather than frugivorous birds. Frame this as a mammalian seed-dispersal adaptation rather than a failure to attract birds.\n\nTaxonomic Conventions:\n\nThe Issue: Lucuma campechiana is highlighted as the primary name, with Pouteria campechiana mentioned as secondary.\n\nThe Fix: While Sapotaceae taxonomy has shifted across historical treatments, Pouteria campechiana (HBK) Baehni remains the standard accepted botanical name across North American hor",
